====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# To be or make a bridge over something.
#: {{ux|en|With enough cable, we can '''bridge''' this gorge.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1947|month=January and February|author=H. A. Vallance|title=The Sea Wall at Dawlish|journal=Railway Magazine|page=18|text=On this occasion, the damage was far more serious. The sea wall was breached completely for a distance of over 50 yd., and the gap had to be '''bridged''' by a temporary timber viaduct.}}
# To [[span]] as if with a bridge.
#: {{ux|en|The two groups were able to '''bridge''' their differences.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2012|author=Christoper Zara|title=Tortured Artists: From Picasso and Monroe to Warhol and Winehouse, the Twisted Secrets of the World's Most Creative Minds|section=part 1, chapter 1, {{gbooks|q=bridged|id=HV4jKhjzMVYC|pg=28}}
|passage=The brooding, black-clad singer '''bridged''' a stark divide that emerged in the recording industry in the 1950s, as post-Elvis pop singers diverged into two camps and audiences aligned themselves with either the sideburned rebels of rock 'n' roll or the cowboy-hatted twangsters of country music.}}
# {{lb|en|music}} To [[transition]] from one piece or section of music to another without stopping.
#: {{ux|en|We need to '''bridge''' that [[jam]] into "The Eleven".}}
# {{lb|en|computing|communication}} To [[connect]] two or more computer buses, networks etc. with a bridge.
# {{lb|en|wrestling}} To go to the bridge position.
# {{lb|en|roller derby}} To employ the bridge tactic. (See Noun section.)

